(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-medical-imaging-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Principles of Medical Imaging Quiz 2 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the decubitus position used to evaluate?","choices":["Pleural effusion, because gravity lets the fluid around the lung level out and be seen","Free air under the diaphragm, because gravity lets the air rise to the highest point","Bowel obstruction, because gravity separates the air from the fluid within the loops","Aortic calcification, because gravity displaces the overlying bowel gas out of the field"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — fluid in the pleural space moves when the patient is laid on their side. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why does the deck stress giving the radiologist relevant clinical information?","choices":["The radiologist selects the modality, so the history determines which scanner is booked","The radiologist repeats the study otherwise, so the history prevents duplicate imaging","The radiologist has not seen the patient, so the history is what guides their read","The radiologist bills separately, so the indication must be documented for the claim"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the report is only as good as the question it is answering. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are ultrasonography's stated indications?","choices":["Assessment of bony structures, naming the skull, the vertebrae and the long bones","Assessment of aerated structures, naming the lungs, the sinuses and the bowel gas pattern","Assessment of metabolic activity, naming tumour staging, brain function and perfusion","Assessment of moving structures, naming the heart, the vasculature and obstetrics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it records in real time, which is what suits it to motion. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which patient does Professor Reynolds say will get an anterior-posterior chest film rather than a posterior-anterior one?","choices":["A sick inpatient who cannot get out of bed","Any patient in whom cardiomegaly is suspected","Any paediatric patient, because of the lower dose","Any patient having a follow-up rather than a first study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they cannot stand against a wall, so they are filmed anterior-posterior in bed. Which is exactly why reading the view label matters in practice. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:14:21"},{"q":"Which safety issues are named for magnetic resonance imaging?","choices":["Nephrotoxicity and anaphylaxis to iodine, and it is still not recommended in renal impairment","Operator dependence and poor penetration of bone, and it is still not recommended in obesity","Magnetic implanted devices and ferromagnetic projectiles, and it is still not recommended in pregnancy or infants","Cumulative ionizing dose and radiation-sensitive organs, and it is still not recommended in pregnancy or infants"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — no radiation is emitted, but the magnet itself creates hazards. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Into which spaces can contrast be injected, and what is each study called?","choices":["Joints for a venogram, the central nervous system intravenously, and the bladder for a cystogram","Muscle for a myogram, the pleural space directly, and the ureter for an antegrade pyelogram","Bowel for an arthrogram, the epidural space, and the renal pelvis for a cystogram","Joints for an arthrogram, the central nervous system intrathecally, and the bladder for a retrograde pyelogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three routes named besides the intravascular and enteral ones. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How are radiographic projections named?","choices":["In the position of the detector, from the cassette's near edge to its furthest corner","In the direction the beam travels, from what it strikes first to the most distal portion","In the direction the patient faces, from the side nearest the film to the side furthest away","In the order the structures appear, from the most superficial down to the deepest tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — so posterior-anterior means the beam enters the back and exits the front. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which four questions frame the choice of an imaging modality?","choices":["Which modality the radiologist prefers, which is available today, which the insurer covers, and which is quickest","Which modality gives the highest resolution, the largest field of view, the thinnest slices, and the fastest overall scan time for the patient","Which modality best rules the diagnosis in or out, is there a lower-radiation alternative, what is the risk against benefit for this patient, and is contrast needed","Which modality is cheapest, which is fastest, which is closest, and which the patient prefers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those are the four the lecture opens with. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"On a bone scan, why are the kidneys visible?","choices":["Uptake in the kidneys occurs only when renal function is impaired and clearance is slowed","Uptake in the kidneys is normal, and the same is true of tracer on positron emission scans","Uptake in the kidneys indicates the tracer dose given was higher than the study required","Uptake in the kidneys suggests metastatic disease has reached the retroperitoneal organs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck labels renal uptake normal on both studies. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What equipment does fluoroscopy require?","choices":["A gamma camera mounted on a rotating gantry that circles the patient, with a fixed table and a detector housing that does not move","A superconducting magnet requiring dedicated site construction beforehand and high ongoing cost","A handheld transducer connected to a portable processing unit, needing neither a tilting table nor a shielded examination room","An x-ray unit fitted for controlled motion of the source, the imaging sensor and the patient, with a tilting table"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the tube moves freely back and forth to image the patient. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Beyond the heart, what else is better seen on the posterior-anterior chest view?","choices":["Maximum visualisation of the mediastinum, better visualisation of the hila, and well visualised anterior ribs","Maximum visualisation of the diaphragm, better visualisation of free air, and well visualised gastric bubble","Maximum visualisation of the pleura, better visualisation of layering fluid, and well visualised costophrenic angles","Maximum visualisation of the lung, better visualisation of the apices, and well visualised posterior ribs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three are listed alongside the cardiac and dose advantages. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which agent is used for fluoroscopic swallow studies, and how is the study performed?","choices":["Oral technetium, with sequential gamma camera images taken as the patient swallows","Oral barium, with sequential x-rays taken as the patient swallows it","Oral gadolinium, with sequential magnetic resonance images taken as the patient swallows","Intravenous iohexol, with sequential computed tomography images taken during swallowing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — real-time imaging is what makes fluoroscopy suit a swallow study. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient has free air under the diaphragm. Which history would make perforation NOT the explanation?","choices":["Recent upper gastrointestinal endoscopy without biopsy","A barium swallow performed the previous week","Chronic constipation with a heavy stool burden","Recent laparoscopic surgery, with the gas not yet absorbed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — insufflation of the abdomen puts gas there deliberately. With belly pain, fever, nausea and vomiting and no recent surgery, it is perforation. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:22:55"},{"q":"Which agent is named for intravenous contrast in computed tomography, and what is it?","choices":["Omnipaque (iohexol), a radioactive form of iodine given into the vein","Visipaque (iodixanol), a barium salt suspension given into the vein","Gadolinium, a paramagnetic metal chelate given into the vein","Gastrografin, a water-soluble iodinated agent given into the vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same agent is used, at higher concentration, for angiography. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which two of the five basic densities cannot be distinguished from one another on a conventional radiograph?","choices":["Fat and air, which both appear black on the image","Calcium and metal, which both absorb all of the beam","Fat and calcium, which occupy adjacent points on the scale","Fluid and soft tissue, which have the same density"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that limitation is one reason computed tomography expands the grey scale. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"On the Hounsfield scale, where water is zero, which tissue sits at roughly minus forty to minus one hundred and twenty?","choices":["Air, which is why it reads darker than every other tissue on the scan","Fat, which is why it reads darker than soft tissue but lighter than air","Soft tissue, which is why it reads darker than fat but lighter than water","Bone, which is why it reads brighter than soft tissue but darker than metal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fat is negative, but nowhere near as negative as air. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is a posterior-anterior chest film oriented when you look at it?","choices":["As if you were looking at the patient's feet, with their left side on your right","As if you were looking down from above, with their right side nearest the top","As if the patient were standing in front of you, with their right side on your left","As if the patient were standing behind you, with their right side on your right"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same convention as facing a patient in the room. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which programmable magnetic resonance technique is named as useful in stroke?","choices":["Diffusion-weighted imaging, which evaluates the diffusion of water within tissue","Fat-suppressed imaging, which removes the signal from adipose tissue in the field","Post-processing windowing, which re-displays the raw data over a narrower range","Gadolinium-enhanced imaging, which highlights vascular structures after injection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the scanner can also be programmed to evaluate blood velocity. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is a computed tomography image composed of, and what is each unit assigned?","choices":["A matrix of thousands of tiny squares called pixels, each assigned a computed tomography number from −1000 to +1000 in Hounsfield units","A matrix of thousands of tiny cubes called voxels, each assigned a greyscale signal intensity on a fixed scale running from zero to two hundred and fifty five","A series of two-dimensional projections, each one assigned its own radiodensity value as measured in millisieverts","A hydrogen map, each point assigned a relaxation time in milliseconds reflecting the local chemical environment of that tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — water is assigned a Hounsfield number of zero by convention. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"On a T2-weighted image, how does tissue with high water content appear?","choices":["Bright, and the examples given are fat, oedema, infection, blood and cerebrospinal fluid","Dark, and the examples given are fat, oedema, infection, blood and cerebrospinal fluid","Bright, and the examples given are cortical bone, tendon, ligament and calcified plaque","Dark, and the examples given are cortical bone, tendon, ligament and calcified plaque"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — remember T2 as the sequence where water is white. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What did Professor Reynolds say about the carcinogenicity of contrast media, and what does it justify?","choices":["None of them is carcinogenic, which is why contrast is used freely","Only the nuclear medicine tracers are carcinogenic, because they are radioactive","It is all carcinogenic","Only the iodinated agents are carcinogenic, which is why gadolinium is preferred"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is exactly why the diagnostic approach asks whether the study can be done without contrast. She tied the risk straight back to the four framing questions. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 27:38"},{"q":"How is radiation dose defined and expressed?","choices":["Ionizing energy delivered per exposure, in millisieverts only, where one millisievert is one milliwatt","Ionizing energy absorbed per unit of mass, in grays or milligrays, where one gray is one joule per kilogram","Ionizing energy emitted per unit of time, in grays or milligrays, where one gray is one joule per second","Ionizing energy absorbed per unit of volume, in sieverts only, where one sievert is one joule per litre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and equivalent dose is then expressed in sieverts or millisieverts. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which three standard planes are used in cross-sectional imaging, and how does each divide the body?","choices":["Axial into upper and lower, coronal into anterior and posterior, sagittal into right and left","Axial into anterior and posterior, coronal into right and left, sagittal into upper and lower","Axial into right and left, coronal into upper and lower, sagittal into anterior and posterior","Axial into upper and lower, coronal into right and left, sagittal into anterior and posterior"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — axial is also called transverse and is the commonest of the three. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"In which units is radiation measured?","choices":["Tesla and gauss","Hertz and decibels","Millisieverts and milligrays","Hounsfield units and pixels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — x-ray radiation is low compared with other radiographic modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which term names an angiographic study of the veins rather than the arteries?","choices":["Arthrogram, performed as either a computed tomography arthrogram or a magnetic one","Venogram, performed as either a magnetic resonance venogram or a computed tomography venogram","Doppler study, performed as either a colour Doppler examination or a duplex examination","Coronary angiogram, performed as either a diagnostic catheterisation or an intervention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same modalities, aimed at the venous side. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the stated trade-off of an open magnetic resonance scanner?","choices":["Decreased quality of imaging, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Increased scan duration, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Increased radiation dose, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Decreased soft tissue contrast only, with spatial resolution otherwise preserved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is available, but the deck flags the loss of quality. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"On the deck's labelled pelvic radiograph, how are bone, gas and the prosthesis described?","choices":["Bone is radiolucent, gas is radiopaque, and the metal prosthesis is very lucent","Bone is very opaque, gas is radiopaque, and the metal prosthesis is radiolucent","Bone is radiopaque, gas is very opaque, and the metal prosthesis is radiolucent","Bone is radiopaque, gas is radiolucent, and the metal prosthesis is very opaque"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — one image showing three of the five densities at once. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does the deck advise when a radiology report comes back vague?","choices":["Refer the patient to the relevant specialist","Contact the radiologist to discuss the patient","Repeat the study using a different modality","Order the next test in the diagnostic sequence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the relationship is described as interprofessional and team-based. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What must be assessed before a magnetic resonance study with contrast, and why?","choices":["Thyroid function, because gadolinium chelates iodine and can precipitate a thyroid storm","Nothing beforehand, because gadolinium is inert and is excreted unchanged in the bile","Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine, because renal function matters primarily for clearance","Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine, because gadolinium is more nephrotoxic than iodine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — gadolinium can damage kidneys, though less than iodinated contrast. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How are magnetic resonance images generated?","choices":["A pulse of sound leaves the transducer; the echo returning from tissue is timed and plotted","A varying magnetic field aligns hydrogen atoms; releasing it produces radio waves the computer maps","A varying magnetic field aligns iron atoms; releasing it produces heat the computer measures","A rotating x-ray beam passes through tissue; detectors measure how much of it is absorbed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck calls the resulting images essentially hydrogen maps. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
